===United States vs Colombia===
The match between the United States and Colombia saw the United States go ahead with just over 10 minutes to play in the first half, when Colombia defender [[Andrés Escobar]] turned a United States cross into his own net. The United States extended their lead early in the second half through [[Earnie Stewart]], while Colombia could only manage a last-minute consolation goal and ended up losing 2–1. The result was the first United States's win in a FIFA World Cup match since the historic [[United States v England (1950 FIFA World Cup)|United States vs. England in 1950]] and meant Colombia's chances of qualifying were no longer in their own hands; they would need to beat Switzerland and hope the United States would beat Romania to stand any chance of qualifying as one of the four best third-placed teams. Escobar was blamed for the result by Colombians and was murdered shortly after returning to the country.<ref>{{Cite web|url = https://www.90min.com/posts/6059408-andres-escobar-the-world-cup-own-goal-that-killed-the-colombia-defender|title = Andres Escobar: The World Cup Own Goal That Killed the Colombia Defender|date = May 13, 2018|access-date = August 12, 2020|archive-date = August 31, 2020|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20200831074619/https://www.90min.com/posts/6059408-andres-escobar-the-world-cup-own-goal-that-killed-the-colombia-defender|url-status = dead}}</ref>

